    <!--
        ScrollViewerScrim - deliberately has NO style here, and that is a decision, not an omission.

        u-021 nearly gave it one: it is win:-only in Themes/Generic.xaml (4136), the gate flags it,
        and its OnApplyTemplate dereferences GetTemplateChild("TopScrim") before the null guard four
        lines below, so with no template it THROWS rather than measuring 0x0. A style looks like the
        obvious fix.

        It is not, because a template only clears the FIRST of the two blockers that
        Xaml/ScrollViewerScrimStub.cs already had written down. The second:
        ElementCompositionPreview.GetScrollViewerManipulationPropertySet is NotImplemented on Uno
        Skia, and that property set is the ONLY input of both expression animations that drive the
        two gradients. Giving the real control a template would build it, template it, and then
        leave it driven by nothing - and this parcel is build-only (no account, no launch), so
        "driven by nothing" could not be told apart from "throws on the first scroll".

        So the inert stub stays, the real Controls/ScrollViewerScrim.cs stays OUT of the subset, and
        no style is written for it: a style for a class whose DefaultStyleKey is never set would
        never apply, which is the same unreachable-style mistake as DefaultChatTextBoxStyle below.
        The way back is the one the stub already prescribes: this style PLUS a scroll source that
        exists here (ScrollViewer.ViewChanged, which does fire), in one pass, verified on screen.
        What is lost meanwhile is the gradient, and only the gradient.
    -->




    <!--
        ColorPicker + ColorTextBox - the colour picker of the backgrounds page ("set a colour"),
        used by Views/Popups/BackgroundPopup.xaml and Views/Popups/ChooseColorPopup.xaml. u-032.

        These two came in with u-011 and BOTH shipped invisible on bf1e004: each keeps
        DefaultStyleKey = typeof(itself) and each one's only <Style TargetType> in Themes/Generic.xaml
        (3813 and 83) is win:-prefixed, so on Skia neither resolves a template and both measure 0x0.
        Nobody saw it because the parcel was verified by building, not by opening the page. Found by
        tools/check-default-style-keys.py, which is the whole reason that gate exists.

        ColorPicker's OnApplyTemplate is properly null-guarded (Controls/ColorPicker.cs:53, :73), so
        this is the silent form of the defect, not the throwing form ScrollViewerScrim has.

        WHY THE TEMPLATE BELOW IS SHORTER THAN UPSTREAM'S 268 LINES, and this is the one decision
        here worth reading:

        Upstream's style is ~35 lines of ColorPicker's own template plus ~200 lines of an INLINE
        <Slider.Template> - a verbatim copy of WinUI's default Slider template - and that inline copy
        is where all 23 {ThemeResource Slider*} keys come from. Those keys are the risk: this parcel
        is build-only, and a resource reference that resolves to nothing takes the whole application's
        resource tree down AT STARTUP, which a green build cannot show. I tried to prove them the way
        PopupInfo's keys were proven in u-021 and COULD NOT: Uno's Fluent resource keys are not stored
        as literals in Uno.UI.dll / Uno.UI.FluentTheme*.dll, and the control for that measurement is
        that keys known to resolve (DefaultTextBoxStyle, TextControlForeground) come back absent too.
        Unproven, not proven-absent - but not something to ship blind.

        So the inline Slider template is dropped and ValueSlider falls back to Uno's OWN default
        Slider style, which IS proven to work in this app: BackgroundPopup.xaml:861 - the same popup -
        already draws a plain <Slider>, as do SettingsStoragePage and SettingsAppearancePage, both of
        which were seen on screen with the real account (FALTA 2.4). The result references ZERO
        resource keys, so there is nothing left to resolve and nothing left to take down.

        WHAT CHANGES ON SCREEN, honestly: the brightness slider keeps its meaning - ColorPicker.cs:154
        does ValueSlider.Foreground = gradient, and WinUI's/Uno's default Slider paints its decrease
        track from Foreground, so the gradient still shows - but it is painted along the filled part
        of a standard Fluent track rather than across a full-width rounded bar, and the thumb is the
        standard Fluent thumb rather than the black/white ringed circle that matches SpectrumThumb.
        Spectrum, its thumb and the colour maths are untouched. If someone wants upstream's exact
        slider back, that is the 200 lines plus ONE screen to confirm the 23 keys resolve - it is a
        look, and it costs a verification this parcel could not do.

        ColorTextBox needs no template of its own: it derives from TextBox and calls no
        GetTemplateChild, and upstream's style is nothing but BasedOn DefaultTextBoxStyle. The
        identical non-win: line already exists three rows below it in Generic.xaml for SuggestTextBox,
        which is live here today - so this is that same proven line, for a sibling class.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="controls:ColorPicker">
        <Setter Property="Padding"
                Value="12,0" />
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="controls:ColorPicker">
                    <Grid>
                        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                            <RowDefinition />
                            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                        </Grid.RowDefinitions>
                        <Border x:Name="Spectrum"
                                CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius}">
                            <Canvas>
                                <Canvas.Background>
                                    <LinearGradientBrush StartPoint="0,0"
                                                         EndPoint="0,1">
                                        <GradientStop Color="#00FFFFFF"
                                                      Offset="0" />
                                        <GradientStop Color="#FFFFFFFF"
                                                      Offset="1" />
                                    </LinearGradientBrush>
                                </Canvas.Background>

                                <Border x:Name="SpectrumThumb"
                                        UseLayoutRounding="False"
                                        Width="20"
                                        Height="20"
                                        CornerRadius="10"
                                        BorderThickness="2"
                                        BorderBrush="Black">
                                    <Border CornerRadius="8"
                                            BorderThickness="2"
                                            BorderBrush="White" />
                                </Border>
                            </Canvas>
                        </Border>
                        <Border x:Name="SpectrumBorder"
                                BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                                BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                                CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius}" />

                        <Slider x:Name="ValueSlider"
                                IsDirectionReversed="True"
                                IsThumbToolTipEnabled="False"
                                Margin="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                                BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                                BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                                Grid.Row="1" />
                    </Grid>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <Style TargetType="controls:ColorTextBox"
           BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultTextBoxStyle}" />


    <!--
        ================================================================================
        u-016, LATENT-1 (DefaultChatTextBoxStyle): NOT ADDED, and the reason is worth more
        than the style would have been.

        u-015 flagged it as "declare a non-win twin now so the next batch does not hit it",
        and that cannot be done here - not because it is awkward, but because the style
        cannot be written yet:

            <win:Style x:Key="DefaultChatTextBoxStyle"
                       TargetType="controls:FormattedTextBox">     Common/CommonStyles.xaml:20

        TargetType names Telegram.Controls.FormattedTextBox, which is NOT in the compiled
        subset (0 hits in Telegram.Linux.csproj), so a copy of this style here would not
        compile at all: a Style whose TargetType is a type the build does not contain is a
        hard XAML error, not a silently ignored one.

        And it will not simply arrive with the next parcel either. FormattedTextBox derives
        from RichEditBox (FormattedTextBox.cs:62), and RichEditBox.Document is the headline
        entry of PORTING.md 6's unimplemented list - FALTA 1.8 scopes that whole editor to
        its own tanda precisely because it is "rehacer el editor sobre otra base", not a
